  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1137 AM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThe upper-level high remains in place over southern New Mexico and \nthat will bring record high temperatures for much of the lowlands \nfor late August. Several lowland locations will see temperatures \nabove the triple digit mark, as indicated by the Heat Advisory in \neffect for the Rio Grande Valley of Texas, Dona Ana County, NM, Luna \nCounty, NM, parts of the Tularosa Basin and White Sands Missile \nRange until 9 PM. There will be enough orographic lift for a couple \nthunderstorms in the mountains this afternoon, especially in the \nSacramento Mountains, but any measurable precipitation will be low.\n\nFor Wednesday, the high will drift slightly to the west, but it will \ncontinue to provide the region with another day of near-record \nhigh temperatures. Conditions for the lowlands should be dry, but \nthe 12Z HRRR run is increasing precipitation coverage to the \nlowlands Wednesday night. I am a little skeptical that rain will \nreach that far south into the lowlands Wednesday night, but on \nThursday, both the GFS and the ECMWF have precipitable water \nvalues for the lowlands around 1-1.2\", giving the lowlands a \nslight (20-30%) chance for precipitation on Thursday \nafternoon/evening. The upper-level high should tamp measurable \nprecipitation down, though. Friday's PWAT values are about the \nsame, though I also expect the upper-level high to suppress rain \namounts for the lowlands as well on Friday.\n\nFor the weekend, the high pressure system is expected to weaken and \nshift eastward. This will cool temperatures down a notch (but \nstill above average), and better conditions for isolated (20-40%) \nthunderstorm development will be present on Saturday and Sunday \nfor the region. As August transitions to September, both extended \nmodels still indicate we will have some monsoonal flow enter the \nregion, which should increase our rain chances for early next \nweek. We will continue to monitor what the extended GFS and ECMWF \nruns indicate.",
